[1. Complete Learning Roadmap]
To master Spring Security, we will traverse these topics in this specific order. This path ensures you don't just "paste code" but understand why it works.
 * Foundations & The Default Magic: Authentication vs. Authorization, and what Spring Security does out-of-the-box.
 * The Security Filter Chain: Understanding the architecture (how requests are intercepted).
 * Custom Configuration: Moving away from defaults using SecurityFilterChain bean.
 * User Management (UserDetailsService): Loading users from memory and databases.
 * Password Encoding (PasswordEncoder): Why we never store plain text passwords (hashing, salting).
 * Authorization (RBAC): Restricting access based on Roles and Authorities.
 * Method-Level Security: Securing specific Java methods (@PreAuthorize).
 * The Filter Architecture: Creating custom filters and understanding the filter order.
 * CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery): What it is and when to disable it.
 * CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing): Allowing frontend frameworks (React/Angular) to talk to your backend.
 * Session Management: Controlling how long a user stays logged in.
 * Stateless Authentication (JWT): JSON Web Tokens for modern REST APIs.
 * OAuth2 & OpenID Connect: Logging in with Google/GitHub.
 * Production Hardening: Headers, HTTPS, and auditing.
[2. Today’s Topic]
Topic 1: Foundations (Authentication vs. Authorization) & The "Hello World" of Security
[3. Concept Explanation]
Spring Security handles two main jobs. If you mix these up, your application will be vulnerable.
 * Authentication (AuthN): The answer to "Who are you?"
 * Authorization (AuthZ): The answer to "What are you allowed to do?"
The Analogy: The Corporate Office Building
Imagine a high-security office building.
 * Authentication (The Receptionist):
   You walk into the lobby. The receptionist asks for your ID badge. They check your face against the photo. If it matches, they confirm: "Okay, you are indeed John Doe." This is Authentication.
 * Authorization (The Keycard Access):
   Now you try to enter the "Server Room." You tap your badge. The reader denies access because you belong to the "HR Department," not "IT." Later, you try to enter the "Cafeteria," and the door opens. This is Authorization.
Spring Security is simply the Receptionist (checking credentials) and the Door Locks (checking permissions) for your web application.
[4. Why This Matters in Real Projects]
In a real project, if you have Authentication but no Authorization, any logged-in user (even a junior intern) could delete the production database or view the CEO's salary.
If you have Authorization but weak Authentication, a hacker can pretend to be the CEO (spoofing identity) and bypass your checks.
You need both to protect data integrity and user privacy.
[5. Code Example]
For our first step, we will see the "Magic" of Spring Security. We will create a simple Spring Boot application and add the security dependency. We will write zero security configuration code, yet the app will be secured.
Prerequisites: A standard Spring Boot project (use start.spring.io) with Spring Web.
1. pom.xml (The Dependency)
Add this to your Maven dependencies. This single dependency changes everything.
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
</dependency>

2. HelloController.java (The Protected Resource)
A simple REST endpoint we want to protect.
package com.example.securitydemo.controller;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/hello")
    public String sayHello() {
        return "Welcome to the secure area! If you see this, you are logged in.";
    }
}

3. SecurityDemoApplication.java (Main Class)
Standard Spring Boot main class.
package com.example.securitydemo;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class SecurityDemoApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(SecurityDemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}

[6. Line-by-Line Code Explanation]
You might be thinking, "Where is the security code?" That is the power of Spring Boot's "Convention over Configuration." Let's analyze what happens when you run this.
The pom.xml dependency
 * spring-boot-starter-security: As soon as this JAR is detected on the "classpath" (in your project), Spring Boot triggers its Auto-Configuration.
 * What happens automatically?
   * It locks down every single URL in your application.
   * It generates a default user called user.
   * It generates a random UUID password and prints it in the application console logs.
   * It creates a default HTML Login Page.
The HelloController.java
 * @RestController: Marks this class as a handler for HTTP requests.
 * @GetMapping("/hello"): Maps HTTP GET requests to /hello.
 * The Flow:
   * You open your browser and go to http://localhost:8080/hello.
   * Before your request reaches the sayHello() method, Spring Security creates a "Wall" (a Filter).
   * The Filter sees you are not logged in (Unauthenticated).
   * It halts the request and redirects you to /login.
   * You see a login form (automatically generated by Spring).
   * You enter username user and the password from your console logs.
   * If correct, Spring creates a Session and lets you through to the string "Welcome to the secure area...".
The Console Output
When you run the app, look closely at the logs. You will see a line like this:
Using generated security password: 4a2b3c...
This is your temporary "Boarding Pass."
[7. Key Takeaways]
 * Security by Default: Spring Security is "secure by default." If you add the library, it assumes you want to lock everything down immediately.
 * Zero Config: You don't need to write a SecurityConfig class to get basic protection.
 * The Interceptor: Security sits in front of your Controller. Your controller code doesn't even know security exists. This is called "Decoupling."
